Why Power Conversion Creates Specialized Wafer Demand
Electric vehicle traction systems and charging equipment are increasing the attention paid to high-voltage switching losses. Silicon wafers remain established for many discrete devices, while silicon carbide offers properties relevant to demanding breakdown voltages and operating temperatures. Customers evaluate the total conversion system, not semiconductor material in isolation.

Wafer diameter expansion can improve the number of die per processed disk, but only if defect density, mechanical behavior, epitaxial growth and fabrication tools are qualified together. A nominally larger wafer does not automatically lower cost per functional power device. Crystal defects and yield variability can reverse the anticipated savings.

