Wafer yield must be evaluated at device level
A substrate can pass dimensional inspection yet produce disappointing die yield if localized crystal defects intersect high-voltage device structures. Manufacturers assess wafer maps, defect distribution and process responses across different epitaxial recipes. An accepted wafer specification should therefore be supported by a data-sharing process that connects incoming material quality to electrical yield and field reliability.

Supplier concentration affects automotive program risk
An inverter platform can remain in production for many model years. Changing a wafer substrate late in a qualification cycle may require renewed tests of breakdown voltage, gate behavior, leakage and long-term stress performance. Dual-source strategy is meaningful only where process compatibility and independent qualification have been demonstrated, not simply where multiple vendors claim similar material dimensions.

